{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's Lexical Divergence Metric is a cross-run analog of a paper-turing test: it measures whether the check looks different, not whether it is independent. Its own countercondition concedes the flaw — a checker can use synonyms to mask restatement of the same logical path. But the proposed remedy (counting the intersection of cited source IDs) does not fix this: a checker can cite disjoint source IDs yet still mechanically re-walk the author's derivation without re-deriving anything, or cite the same canonical sources in a different surface form. Conversely, genuine independent verification of a narrow claim may necessarily cite the same single artifact (the countercondition admits domains with only one possible supporting evidence). So the metric both under-detects restatement and over-penalizes genuine verification. The problem asks what evidence distinguishes genuine independent verification from restatement, and where a claim can be tested by counting or execution, say exactly what to count or execute; 'count citation-ID overlap' is neither sufficient (disjoint IDs ≠ independence) nor necessary (same IDs ≠ restatement).","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues that separating engines may decrease reliability, but its positive criterion for verification — 'Depth of Trace', whether the checker identifies the specific step of failure — is available to and diagnostic of a checker regardless of whether it is the same or a separate engine. The argument therefore never establishes that separation is harmful in kind; it only asserts a risk (rubber-stamping) that is itself a failure of independent checking, not a feature of separation. Worse, it offers no countable/executionable test for when 'Depth of Trace' has occurred, so per the problem's demand for what to count or execute it gives no evidence rule that would distinguish genuine independent verification from restatement within a single run's record.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_003","attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's Cross-Domain Isomorphism test conflates representational change with independent verification. A translation into a different formal language can be a mechanical, semantics-preserving transformation (e.g., parsing a first-order sentence into a proof assistant) that carries the author's error straight through; its own counterconditions concede this. Its evidence rule — Jaccard similarity of tokens between claim and intermediate representation below 0.3 — measures lexical dissimilarity of the representation, not whether the checker independently re-derived the result. A checker can satisfy the threshold by choosing any alternative notation while reproducing the author's logic exactly; and a checker who independently verifies in the same domain but by a different proof path would fail the test. So the test neither identifies genuine independent verification nor reliably excludes restatement, and it does not answer the problem's request to say exactly what to count or execute to separate the two.","counterexample":null},{"target_alias":"SRC_004","attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's 'Cognitive Mirroring' argument proves too much in a way that defeats the requested evidence test. It frames confirmation bias as a structural property of autoregressive conditioning on prior tokens, but its own countercondition — a clean-slate checker context that erases the author's generation history — is exactly how a same-engine second pass is normally run, and the target gives no countable or executionable marker to decide whether a given same-engine check is still under 'gravity' or has escaped it. Without such a marker the claim that single-engine genuine verification is impossible is unfalsifiable in the record and provides no way, as the problem requires, to distinguish genuine independent verification from restatement within a single run; it also ignores that a separate engine can equally rubber-stamp, so separation is asserted as necessary without an in-record discriminator.","counterexample":null}]}